The embedder can keep a page from every discard but an external one, for state Chrome doesn't track: a page reached
by a form post (reloading it would send the form again), a page with an unload prompt, a page that started a
download still running. DiscardEligibilityPolicy asks one hook the embedder sets at startup,
g_ardia_keeps_page(const PageNode*) (the same shape and name as Windows' chromium-win-discard-asks-the-host.patch),
right after its checks that the page is a loaded tab and before the early answer for an external or
frozen-with-growing-memory discard, and refuses a vetoed page outright as kOptedOut, the reason Chrome already gives a
page an enterprise policy keeps, recording the decision in Chromium's discard histograms. Performance manager depends
on no Ardia code and no header changes: ArdiaCore declares the hook itself (ac_discard_veto.mm) and decides what it
vetoes. An external discard (the tab menu's Sleep Tab) is never vetoed. Every other protection is Chrome's. New unit
test: TestEmbedderVetoKeepsAllButExternal, which covers the frozen-with-growing-memory discard.

